all right let's bring in kerry mccarthy  she's the shadow of climate change  minister kerry mccarthy good morning to  you thank you for being with us just  pick up on what you heard there from  jacob rees-mogg i mean saying the nickel  surgeon's always moaning what's that  going to do trying to keep the union  together well it's obviously very little  in terms of having a constructive  relationship obviously labour supports  the union we want to work with nicola  sturgeon we don't support her calls for  independence but i think they're sort of  name-calling just saying she's  attention-seeking  for pursuing the calls that she's been  elected to to do as an snp politician i  think it's it's pretty juvenile and i  think sadly that's the way the  conservative party leadership contest  seems to be going doesn't it well  personal attacks at some point let's  pick up another big story from this  morning there's bp results uh huge  profits being made obviously labour  party heavily campaigned for a windfall  tax on energy profits that has been  agreed to in part  what happens next to try and make sure  that people's cost of living is is dealt  with well i think people who find it  quite shocking that they're making  profits i think these are the highest  quarterly profits recorded for 14 years  and we've seen the same from shell we've  seen the same from centrica as well so  there's clearly a pattern that they're  doing very well out of this situation  and yet we've got this cost of living  crisis and it's quite scary the extent  to which bills are predicted to go up in  october and then again in january you're  talking about predictions of over three  and a half thousand pounds for for  typical bills and people are just really  really struggling to make ends meet now  that you're hearing stories about people  just giving up on their prepayment  meters and it might be quite small debts  it might be that they just can't afford  15 20 pounds but that's the difference  between being able to heat their homes  and you know turn the lights on and not  so certainly labour's calling for um as  an immediate um  an immediate measure um  scrapping vat on fuel bills for now um  but we're also calling on the government  to bring in an emergency budget and  again it goes back to what i was saying  about what's currently going on in the  conservative party because we can't wait  for we know the bills will be going up  at the start of october we can't wait  for a new prime minister in september to  then assemble a cabinet to then decide  what they're going to do they're in  government now they're meant to be  getting on with dealing with the cost of  living crisis and yet they're not yeah  let's move on to uh what we're seeing on  on some of the streets around the  country people uh striking calling for a  greater um  a wage increase i've seen another  another couple of labour party members  and shadow cabinet members on picket  lines what is the ruling in the labour  party what does kirsten want his  ministers to do when it comes to being  on picket lines well our general line is  we very we support the right to strike  and if unions have taken them balloted  their members and taken a democratic  decision to go on strike then that is  yeah they're well within their rights to  do that we also support the  right of you know we think it's right  for mps to go out and talk to the people  that they represent which is what lisa  and andy was doing this week with a  picture of listening as well doing that  if we can bring it up sorry please  so but really what this comes down to is  the need to actually get a negotiated  deal to get a fair deal for the people  that have gone on strike  and that requires government to actually  step up and encourage people to get  around the negotiating table it  certainly does let's just bring up  though what sir kirsten has said about  the labour party and what they should do  around strike action the labor party in  opposition needs to be the labor party  in power and the government doesn't go  on picket lines i mean this kind of  infighting when the conservative parties  have been disarrayed is not very helpful  to the labour party is it what's going  on i think it's not helpful for the  focus to be on labour's position on this  one clearly it's the government that has  responsibilities you're sitting here  if you look at like grant shapes you  know complete refusal to step into the  room  we're talking about what's happening in  the labor party yeah but i think he's  been quite clear that we do support the  trade union rights we do support workers  um  you know the right that they have to  actually withhold their labor if a  democratic decision has has been maken  and he's been very quite clear um  so why is lisa and andy going to pick  its why was cemetery at pickett's the  other day i mean there are there are  members of the party who want to go and  want to support the strikers more  full-throatedly than secure stamina  wants to why is there that disconnect  well i think i mean keir said yeah as i  understand it was  he was  spoken to lisa and he was quite relaxed  about the the fact that she went out to  talk to the people that she represents  the difference with sam is that he  actually did a whole media round and  came up with policies that hadn't been  cleared by the party and that's just not  something that you do if you're in the  shadow front bench team you have to have  collective responsibility it puts your  colleagues in a very difficult position  if people are just freelancing it and i  think sam did know that that's what he  was doing so that's why he was sad but  you know there was a threat to sack  other people i don't think he's going to  sack lisa nanny but it's very clear what  he says isn't it he doesn't want you  going to the pickets i mean would you do  that to pick up i think there's a  performative element of standing on the  picket line holding up signs when you're  not the one on strike but i think  there's a difference between that and  actually going out and talking to  people and supporting you know i i've  spoken to people i've not been on a  picket line but i've been i've had  conversations with people that are going  out on strike and people um some of the  unions that are talking about possible  strike action in the autumn and i think  that's that's very much my job as a an  mp to do that do you think it's a vote  loser to be full-throatedly behind the  union for the labour party i would like  to think that people recognize the  importance of working people's rights i  mean they were very hard fought for and  particularly when you have  a situation where you've got  skyrocketing inflation you've got the  cost of living crisis that we've already  spoken about it's only fair for people  to be allowed to um ask for that their  wages would um help them meet some of  the cost of that so i think that's  that's incredibly legitimate i think  what's not helpful is when  it would be seen as labour in the  forefront of this as a and taking  attention off the government so  grant chaps would love it to be labour  mps on picket lines with the rail  workers and that be the story because  that means that he can abdicate any  sense of responsibility for  being you know being the one that ought  to be stepping in and finding a solution

